              <h2>What Makes an App Truly "Montessori"? (4 Core Principles)</h2>
              <p>
                Of course, Maria Montessori valued hands-on physical materials above all else. Yet when families do use
                screens (whether on a long flight, a rainy afternoon, or during quiet family reading time), a digital
                experience can honor Montessori principles if it respects four rules:
              </p>
              <ol>
                <li><strong>Reality &amp; Nature Over Fantasy Chaos (Cosmic Education):</strong> Montessori education
                  grounds children in the real world, focusing on real animals, botany, geography, and natural
                  phenomena, because reality is already full of wonder.</li>
                <li><strong>Child-Led Pace (Follow the Child):</strong> There are no countdown timers forcing a child to
                  hurry, and no rigid linear levels locking them out of topics they are curious about.</li>
                <li><strong>Intrinsic Motivation (No Extrinsic Bribes):</strong> In a Montessori classroom, learning is
                  its own reward. Truly Montessori apps skip virtual coins, loot boxes, and flashy confetti explosions.
                </li>
                <li><strong>A Prepared, Peaceful Environment:</strong> Clean visuals, gentle acoustics, and zero
                  third-party ads or pop-ups.</li>
              </ol>

