                    <span class="app-pick-pill neutral">Best Sensorimotor Logic for Toddlers</span>
                    <h3 style="margin-top: 0.6rem !important;">5. Busy Shapes</h3>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <div class="app-specs-bar">
                  <span><i class="fa-solid fa-child"></i> Ages 2–6</span>
                  <span><i class="fa-solid fa-tag"></i> Standalone Purchase</span>
                  <span><i class="fa-solid fa-shapes"></i> Self-Correcting Puzzles</span>
                </div>
                <p>
                  A cornerstone of Montessori education is <em>control of error</em>: designing materials that allow a
                  child to see and correct their own mistakes independently, without an adult stepping in. <a
                    href="https://www.edokiacademy.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Busy Shapes</a>
                  applies this principle (alongside Jean Piaget's research on sensorimotor development) to minimalist
                  shape-matching workspaces for young children.
                </p>
                <p>
                  <strong>Why it stands out:</strong> As children slide shapes into matching holes, the workspace gently
                  evolves with new physical puzzles such as puddles, moving walls, and color-changing blocks. The
                  interface is completely wordless, visually uncluttered, and adapts smoothly to your child's skill
                  level, building spatial reasoning and quiet concentration.
                </p>
              </div>

              <!-- 6. TINYBOP THE EARTH & PLANTS -->
              <div class="app-review-card">
                <div class="app-review-header">
                  <div>
                    <span class="app-pick-pill neutral">Best for Elementary "Cosmic Education" Science</span>
                    <h3 style="margin-top: 0.6rem !important;">6. Tinybop (The Earth &amp; Plants)</h3>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <div class="app-specs-bar">
                  <span><i class="fa-solid fa-child"></i> Ages 4–10</span>
                  <span><i class="fa-solid fa-tag"></i> Standalone App Purchases</span>
                  <span><i class="fa-solid fa-mountain"></i> Geology &amp; Botany</span>
                </div>
                <p>
                  In Montessori elementary classrooms (ages 6–12), children explore what Maria Montessori called
                  <em>Cosmic Education</em>: studying plate tectonics, volcanoes, erosion, weather cycles, and plant
                  botany to understand how our planet works as an interconnected whole. Brooklyn-based studio <a
                    href="https://tinybop.com/apps" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Tinybop</a> captures this
                  spirit wonderfully in its <em>Explorer's Library</em> series.
                </p>
                <p>
                  <strong>Why it stands out:</strong> In <em>The Earth</em>, kids can trigger volcanic eruptions, watch
                  glaciers carve valleys over millennia, and inspect rock layers; in <em>Plants</em>, they watch
                  ecosystems shift across seasons from spring blossoms to winter snow. Tinybop also provides free,
                  expert-written downloadable handbooks for parents to spark rich science conversations together.
                </p>
              </div>

              <!-- 7. DRAGONBOX NUMBERS -->
              <div class="app-review-card">
                <div class="app-review-header">
                  <div>
                    <span class="app-pick-pill neutral">Best Visual Number Rods</span>
                    <h3 style="margin-top: 0.6rem !important;">7. DragonBox Numbers</h3>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <div class="app-specs-bar">
                  <span><i class="fa-solid fa-child"></i> Ages 4–8</span>
                  <span><i class="fa-solid fa-ruler-vertical"></i> Cuisenaire / Montessori Rods</span>
                </div>
                <p>
                  Created by former math teacher Jean-Baptiste Huynh, <a href="https://dragonbox.com/" target="_blank"
                    rel="noopener noreferrer">DragonBox Numbers</a> is one of the smartest early math apps ever made. It
                  turns abstract digits from 1 to 10 (and up to 100) into proportional, colorful characters called
                  "Nooms" that mirror physical Montessori red-and-blue number rods and Cuisenaire blocks.
                </p>
                <p>
                  <strong>Why it stands out:</strong> Instead of drilling children with flashcards, DragonBox lets kids
                  physically stack, slice, compare, and combine rods across four gentle game modes. Children can
                  literally see why two "3" rods equal one "6" rod, developing a rock-solid, intuitive grasp of
                  addition, subtraction, and number sense long before encountering formal worksheets.
                </p>
              </div>

